sec.p:
The epidemiological parameters of resistant strains are defined relative to those of sensitive strains through the cost parameters δs acting on the transmission and clearance rates. The costs of resistance did not depend on whether it was associated with a persistent (P) or colonizer (C) type. Given βSk,1\begin{document}$\beta_{S_{k}, 1}$\end{document} and βSSk,2(k∈{C,P})\begin{document}$\beta_{S_{S_{k}, 2}}(k \in\{C, P\})$\end{document}, we distinguish the cost of resistance on colonization and super-colonization as
βRk,1=(1−δβ,R,1)βSk,1βRk,2=(1−δβ,R,2)βSk,2, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} & \beta_{R_{k}, 1}=\left(1-\delta_{\beta, R, 1}\right) \beta_{S_{k}, 1} \\ & \beta_{R_{k}, 2}=\left(1-\delta_{\beta, R, 2}\right) \beta_{S_{k}, 2},\end{aligned}$$\end{document}
where δβ,R,1 and δβ,R,2 are both included in [0, 1] and measure the cost of resistance on colonization and super-colonization, respectively. Similarly, we assumed that resistance can reduce persistence through an increased clearance rate that depends on whether the host is single- or co-colonized.

We modeled the clearance rates of resistant strains compared to that of sensitive strains as
γRk,1=γSk,1(1+δγ,R,1)γRk,2=γSk,2(1+δγ,R,2) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} & \gamma_{R_{k}, 1}=\gamma_{S_{k}, 1}\left(1+\delta_{\gamma, R, 1}\right) \\ & \gamma_{R_{k}, 2}=\gamma_{S_{k}, 2}\left(1+\delta_{\gamma, R, 2}\right)\end{aligned}$$\end{document}
where δγ,R,1 and δγ,R,2 are the costs of resistance included in [0, ∞].

Overall, we assume that the costs of resistance can occur on transmission and clearance and that they can depend on the colonization status of the hosts. We infered the costs of resistance that are measured by the set of parameters :


PR={mx,δβ,R,1,δβ,R,2,δγ,R,1,δγ,R,2}
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle P_{R}=\left\{m_{x}, \delta_{\beta, R, 1}, \delta_{\beta, R, 2}, \delta_{\gamma, R, 1}, \delta_{\gamma, R, 2}\right\}$$\end{document}



The other parameters were directly obtained from the focal data or from data in similar communities, as broadly described below. The details of the parameter estimation are provided in Supplementary Material S3. Table 1 recapitulates the model parameters.

We further assumed that the number of bacteria in each sample followed a Poisson distribution. The Maximum Likelihood estimator for the number of bacteria is then
η^=∑iki∑idi \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \hat{\eta}=\frac{\sum_{i} k_{i}}{\sum_{i} d_{i}}$$\end{document}
where di is the dilution factor and ki is the number of bacteria counted at dilution di.

Our aim was to use MCMC to estimate the posterior distribution of the 14 parameters:


{λ0,λ1,λ00,λ01,λ10,λ11,μ0,μ1,μ00,μ01,μ10,μ11,ν01,ν10}
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \left\{\lambda_{0}, \lambda_{1}, \lambda_{00}, \lambda_{01}, \lambda_{10}, \lambda_{11}, \mu_{0}, \mu_{1}, \mu_{00}, \mu_{01}, \mu_{10}, \mu_{11}, \nu_{01}, \nu_{10}\right\}$$\end{document}



After initial fits of the model where strains were considered as all equivalent, we used exponential priors with means (in months−1):

We used the mean posterior distribution of the six clearance rates (see below for the transmission rates), which were estimated to:


μ¯0=0.145m−1μ¯1=0.0677m−1μ¯00=0.723m−1μ¯01=0.0978m−1μ¯10=0.308m−1μ¯11=0.340m−1
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \bar{\mu}_{0} & =0.145 \mathrm{~m}^{-1} \\ \bar{\mu}_{1} & =0.0677 \mathrm{~m}^{-1} \\ \bar{\mu}_{00} & =0.723 \mathrm{~m}^{-1} \\ \bar{\mu}_{01} & =0.0978 \mathrm{~m}^{-1} \\ \bar{\mu}_{10} & =0.308 \mathrm{~m}^{-1} \\ \bar{\mu}_{11} & =0.340 \mathrm{~m}^{-1}\end{aligned}$$\end{document}



These parameters are related to the parameters of our model following:


μ¯0=γSC,1μ¯1=γSP,1μ¯00=γSC,1(1+d)μ¯01=γSC,1(1+d)(1−ϵ)μ¯10=γSP,1(1+d)(1−ϵ)μ¯11=γSP,1(1+d)
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \bar{\mu}_{0} & =\gamma_{S_{C}, 1} \\ \bar{\mu}_{1} & =\gamma_{S_{P}, 1} \\ \bar{\mu}_{00} & =\gamma_{S_{C}, 1}(1+d) \\ \bar{\mu}_{01} & =\gamma_{S_{C}, 1}(1+d)(1-\epsilon) \\ \bar{\mu}_{10} & =\gamma_{S_{P}, 1}(1+d)(1-\epsilon) \\ \bar{\mu}_{11} & =\gamma_{S_{P}, 1}(1+d)\end{aligned}$$\end{document}



where d measures the additional clearance from co-colonized hosts (such that γk,2 = γk,1(1 − d)) (k ∈ {S, P}) and ϵ measures niche differentiation (see main text). We used a linear model to estimate the γSC,1,γSP,1,d\begin{document}$\gamma_{S_{C}, 1}, \gamma_{S_{P}, 1}, d$\end{document} and ϵ (the four model parameters) from the mean of the posterior distributions using


log⁡μ―=log⁡γSC,1Xγ,SC+log⁡γSP,1Xγ,SP+log⁡(1+d)Xd+log⁡(1−ϵ)Xϵ
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \log \overline{\boldsymbol{\mu}}=\log \gamma_{S_{C}, 1} \mathbf{X}_{\gamma, \mathbf{S}_{\mathbf{C}}}+\log \gamma_{S_{P}, 1} \mathbf{X}_{\gamma, \mathbf{S}_{\mathbf{P}}}+\log (1+d) \mathbf{X}_{\mathbf{d}}+\log (1-\epsilon) \mathbf{X}_{\epsilon}$$\end{document}



where μ―\begin{document}$\overline{\boldsymbol{\mu}}$\end{document} is the vector of the mean of the posterior for each of the clearance parameters, the Xi,k are binary vectors with value depending on whether μ¯i\begin{document}$\bar{\mu}_{i}$\end{document} contains the corresponding factor k. The parameters γSP,1,γSC,1,d\begin{document}$\gamma_{S_{P}, 1}, \gamma_{S_{C}, 1}, d$\end{document} and ϵ can be directly obtained from the estimated coefficients of the linear model. Solving this linear model, we thus obtained γSP,1=0.06,γSC,1=0.15,d=4.0\begin{document}$\gamma_{S_{P}, 1}=0.06, \gamma_{S_{C}, 1}=0.15, d=4.0$\end{document} and ϵ = 0.65. These estimates result in the clearance parameters presented in Table 1.

We used the mean posterior distribution of the six transmission rates, divided by the frequency of strain 0 (colonizers) or 1 (persisters) in the community, to estimate the transmission rates:


β¯0=0.459m−1β¯1=0.335m−1β¯00=0.300m−1β¯01=0.155m−1β¯10=0.106m−1β¯11=0.167m−1
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{gathered}\bar{\beta}_{0}=0.459 \mathrm{~m}^{-1} \\ \bar{\beta}_{1}=0.335 \mathrm{~m}^{-1} \\ \bar{\beta}_{00}=0.300 \mathrm{~m}^{-1} \\ \bar{\beta}_{01}=0.155 \mathrm{~m}^{-1} \\ \bar{\beta}_{10}=0.106 \mathrm{~m}^{-1} \\ \bar{\beta}_{11}=0.167 \mathrm{~m}^{-1}\end{gathered}$$\end{document}



These parameters are related to the parameters of our model following:


β¯0=βSC,1β¯1=βSP,1β¯00=βSC,1(1−dβ)β¯01=βSC,1(1−dβ)(1+ϵβ)β¯10=βSP,1(1−dβ)(1+ϵβ)β¯11=βSP,1(1−dβ)
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} & \bar{\beta}_{0}=\beta_{S_{C}, 1} \\ & \bar{\beta}_{1}=\beta_{S_{P}, 1} \\ & \bar{\beta}_{00}=\beta_{S_{C}, 1}\left(1-d_{\beta}\right) \\ & \bar{\beta}_{01}=\beta_{S_{C}, 1}\left(1-d_{\beta}\right)\left(1+\epsilon_{\beta}\right) \\ & \bar{\beta}_{10}=\beta_{S_{P}, 1}\left(1-d_{\beta}\right)\left(1+\epsilon_{\beta}\right) \\ & \bar{\beta}_{11}=\beta_{S_{P}, 1}\left(1-d_{\beta}\right)\end{aligned}$$\end{document}



where dβ measures the reduced transmission to already colonized hosts and ϵβ measures niche differentiation acting on transmission. We used a linear model to estimate the βSC,1,βSP,1,dβ\begin{document}$\beta_{S_{C}, 1}, \beta_{S_{P}, 1}, d_{\beta}$\end{document} and ϵβ (the four model parameters) from the mean of the posterior distributions using


log⁡β―=log⁡βSC,1Xβ,SC+log⁡βSP,1Xβ,SP+log⁡(1−dβ)Xd+log⁡(1+ϵβ)Xϵ
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \log \overline{\boldsymbol{\beta}}=\log \beta_{S_{C}, 1} \mathbf{X}_{\beta, \mathbf{S}_{\mathbf{C}}}+\log \beta_{S_{P}, 1} \mathbf{X}_{\beta, \mathbf{S}_{\mathbf{P}}}+\log \left(1-d_{\beta}\right) \mathbf{X}_{\mathbf{d}}+\log \left(1+\epsilon_{\beta}\right) \mathbf{X}_{\epsilon}$$\end{document}



where β¯\begin{document}$\bar{\beta}$\end{document} is the vector of the mean of the posterior for each of the transmission parameters, the Xi,k are binary vectors with value depending on whether β¯i\begin{document}$\bar{\beta}_{i}$\end{document} contains the corresponding factor k. The parameters βSP,1,βSC,1,d\begin{document}$\beta_{S_{P}, 1}, \beta_{S_{C}, 1}, d$\end{document} and ϵβ can be directly obtained from the estimated coefficients of the linear model. Solving this linear model, we thus obtained βSP,1=0.360m−1,βSC,1=0.427m−1,dβ=0.6\begin{document}$\beta_{S_{P}, 1}=0.360 m^{-1}, \beta_{S_{C}, 1}=0.427 m^{-1}, d_{\beta}=0.6$\end{document}. As the term corresponding to niche differentiation on transmission was not significant in the linear model, and in accordance with another study, we considered there was no niche differentiation on transmission (ϵβ = 0.).

The inferred transmission rates in the Swedish cohort were not large enough to reach the observed prevalence of E. coli in our French cohort, perhaps because of differences in epidemiology between Sweden and France. Furthermore, these transmission rates together with the inferred clearance rates led to large difference in competitive ability between persisters and colonizers. We therefore modified the transmission rates to better fit our data. The transmission rates to uncolonized hosts were instead computed to ensure that the P- and C-types (roughly) have the same R0 and that the expected frequency of uncolonized hosts was (roughly) S∗ = 1.7 % as observed in our data. Precisely, we solved:
βSC,1γSC,1=βSP,1γSP,1S∗=(γSC,1+γSP,1)/(βSC,1+βSP,1) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \frac{\beta_{S_{C}, 1}}{\gamma_{S_{C}, 1}} & =\frac{\beta_{S_{P}, 1}}{\gamma_{S_{P}, 1}} \\ S^{*} & =\left(\gamma_{S_{C}, 1}+\gamma_{S_{P}, 1}\right) /\left(\beta_{S_{C}, 1}+\beta_{S_{P}, 1}\right)\end{aligned}$$\end{document}
The second equation corresponds to a rough approximation for the expected frequency of un-colonized hosts. Solving these two equations for βSC,1\begin{document}$\beta_{S_{C}, 1}$\end{document}, and βSP,1\begin{document}$\beta_{S_{P}, 1}$\end{document}, results in βSC,1=8.74m−1\begin{document}$\beta_{S_{C}, 1}=8.74 m^{-1}$\end{document} and βSP,1=3.60m−1\begin{document}$\beta_{S_{P}, 1}=3.60 m^{-1}$\end{document} as presented in Table 1. We finally used the inferred effect of colonization on transmission rates value of, estimated at dβ = 0.6 to compute the values of βSC,2=3.5m−1\begin{document}$\beta_{S_{C}, 2}=3.5 m^{-1}$\end{document} and βSP,1=1.44m−1\begin{document}$\beta_{S_{P}, 1}=1.44 m^{-1}$\end{document}. The final transmission parameters are presented in Table 1.
